Now I have thoroughly read all the required documents. Let me produce my analysis as the domain-skeptic agent. I have a comprehensive understanding of:

1. **Basil** (deepest, 1009 lines): Has temperature optima (25-30°C), but RH data is disease-risk only, soil moisture is field-capacity %, light is PPFD not lux, no duration data anywhere, no recovery thresholds.

2. **Lettuce** (254 lines): Temperature ranges exist but are general references (not_a_risk_threshold per gap registry), RH absent, soil moisture absent, PPFD/DLI from single cultivar/system only.

3. **Gap registry**: Systematically documents that across all 6 crops, virtually every sensor channel lacks direct, transferable, universally applicable evidence for scoring.

My honest domain assessment: only air temperature has a defensible (but still narrow) mapping. Everything else is either a unit mismatch, category error, or absent.

{"agent_id": "agy-domain-skeptic", "role_summary": "Challenge the sensor-to-evidence mapping for horticultural validity and determine whether a numeric environment score is scientifically defensible on this evidence base.", "recommendation": "Only air temperature can be numerically scored, and even then only as a range-membership check (inside/outside 25-30°C for basil, 18-24°C for lettuce) without duration weighting. The other 7 sensor channels cannot produce defensible numeric scores. The product is not an environment score — it is a condition monitor that shows current readings, flags the one or two channels where literature thresholds exist, and surfaces qualitative guidance for everything else.", "why": ["Air temperature is the sole channel where documented optima are directly comparable to sensor output in the same unit, though even here no tolerated-duration data exists, making time-weighted scoring unbacked.", "Air humidity numbers in the corpus are exclusively disease-risk thresholds (basil downy mildew RH ≥85%, leaf wetness ≥4h, night RH >95% for ≥7.5h) — using them as growth-optimum boundaries is a category error the documents explicitly warn against (basil lines 97, 135, 517).", "The lux sensor cannot be compared to PPFD/DLI evidence without a spectrum-dependent conversion the documents forbid (basil line 12); this is not a gap that calibration resolves because the conversion factor changes with light source.", "Soil moisture evidence is either field-capacity ratios (basil line 406), or system-specific VWC from a particular sensor+medium (cherry tomato 10HS in 2:1 soil-sand), both flagged measurement_not_transferable in the registry; a generic capacitive sensor maps to neither.", "CO2 has essentially zero per-crop evidence. Particulate matter and noise have literally none. Noise is not even a recognised horticultural factor.", "The gap registry confirms that across all 6 covered crops, optimum RH, optimum VPD, duration thresholds, and recovery thresholds are universally absent — these are not isolated gaps but structural holes."], "schema_sketch": "-- The schema must enforce the scoring-eligibility verdict per sensor-axis-crop.\n-- This table is the critical guardrail.\n\nCREATE TABLE sensor_axis_eligibility (\n  crop_id          TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES crops(crop_id),\n  sensor_channel   TEXT NOT NULL,  -- e.g. 'air_temperature', 'air_humidity', 'illuminance_lux'\n  scoring_tier     TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (scoring_tier IN (\n    'scorable',        -- direct evidence exists in matching units\n    'advisory',        -- evidence exists but with category/unit/scope mismatch\n    'record_only'      -- no horticultural evidence at all\n  )),\n  mismatch_type    TEXT,           -- NULL if scorable; else 'unit_mismatch', 'category_error', 'no_evidence', 'measurement_basis_mismatch'\n  rationale        TEXT NOT NULL,  -- one-sentence justification\n  UNIQUE(crop_id, sensor_channel)\n);\n\n-- Worked example rows:\n-- ('basil', 'air_temperature',   'scorable',    NULL,                           '25-30°C literature optimum directly comparable to sensor °C')\n-- ('basil', 'air_humidity',      'advisory',    'category_error',               'RH ≥85% is downy mildew risk, not growth optimum')\n-- ('basil', 'illuminance_lux',   'advisory',    'unit_mismatch',                'evidence is PPFD; lux→PPFD requires spectrum-dependent conversion')\n-- ('basil', 'soil_moisture',     'advisory',    'measurement_basis_mismatch',   'evidence is field-capacity %; sensor gives uncalibrated VWC')\n-- ('basil', 'soil_temperature',  'advisory',    'category_error',               'values are stage-specific or disease-management targets')\n-- ('basil', 'co2',               'record_only', 'no_evidence',                  'no per-crop optimum or threshold')\n-- ('basil', 'particulate_matter','record_only', 'no_evidence',                  'no horticultural threshold in corpus')\n-- ('basil', 'noise',             'record_only', 'no_evidence',                  'not a recognised crop growth factor')\n-- For lettuce: air_temperature is scorable (18-24°C general range), all others follow same pattern as basil.\n\n-- The scoring layer queries:\n-- SELECT sensor_channel FROM sensor_axis_eligibility\n-- WHERE crop_id = ? AND scoring_tier = 'scorable';\n-- This returns ONLY air_temperature for most crops.\n\n-- Advisory channels surface section-9 qualitative guidance instead of a number:\nCREATE TABLE qualitative_guidance (\n  crop_id         TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES crops(crop_id),\n  sensor_channel  TEXT NOT NULL,\n  guidance_text   TEXT NOT NULL,   -- from document section 9\n  source_section  TEXT NOT NULL,   -- e.g. 'section_9_user_actions'\n  FOREIGN KEY (crop_id, sensor_channel) REFERENCES sensor_axis_eligibility(crop_id, sensor_channel)\n);", "scorable_axes": ["air_temperature — literature optima (e.g. basil 25-30°C, lettuce 18-24°C) are directly comparable to sensor °C output, though no duration weighting is possible"], "unscorable_axes": ["air_humidity — all RH values are disease-risk thresholds (basil downy mildew ≥85%), not growth optima; scoring them as optima is a documented category error", "illuminance_lux — all evidence is PPFD (μmol/m²/s); lux→PPFD conversion depends on light spectrum and the documents explicitly prohibit this derivation", "soil_moisture — evidence uses field-capacity ratios or system-specific VWC with named sensors in named media, all flagged measurement_not_transferable", "soil_temperature — evidence is stage-specific (germination) or disease-management (hydroponic Pythium 20-24°C), no whole-cycle optimum exists", "co2 — only one basil temperature×CO2 interaction study; no per-crop optimum or threshold for any crop", "particulate_matter — zero horticultural evidence in the entire corpus", "noise — not a recognised plant growth factor in horticultural science; scoring it would be pseudoscience"], "assumptions": ["The hardware sensor outputs raw lux, not PPFD — if it actually outputs PPFD, the light channel would move from unscorable to advisory-with-caveats.", "The documents' own epistemic discipline (refusing to fabricate values) is a feature, not a bug — this project genuinely intends to be evidence-bound rather than hand-wavily useful.", "A dashboard that honestly shows 1 scored axis plus 7 advisory/monitoring channels is viable as a product, i.e. the user can tolerate epistemic honesty."], "risks": ["Labelling 7 of 8 channels unscorable may be perceived as torpedoing the product; stakeholders may override with invented thresholds, which is exactly the failure mode the documents were written to prevent.", "Even air temperature scoring is fragile: without duration data, a momentary excursion triggers the same response as a sustained one, producing nuisance alerts.", "My analysis is corpus-bound: I can only evaluate what these 8 documents contain; well-known horticultural references (e.g. general RH 60-80% for most crops) exist outside this corpus but were deliberately excluded by the researchers, and I cannot assess whether their exclusion was correct."], "cheap_validation": "For each of the 8 sensor channels × 8 crops (64 cells), grep the markdown files for a numeric value in the matching unit (°C for temp, % for RH, lux for light, etc.) that is explicitly labelled as an optimum (적정 or 최적) rather than a disease-risk, experimental, or stage-specific condition. Count how many cells have a qualifying value. My prediction: ≤10 of 64, and all of them are air_temperature or soil_pH — neither of which maps to a sensor that outputs lux, VWC, or ppm.", "change_mind_if": "If someone shows that the hardware light sensor actually outputs calibrated PPFD (not lux), AND the soil moisture sensor is the exact model+medium combination used in the documented studies, then two more axes become scorable and a 3-axis score covering temperature+light+moisture could be defensible."}
